"Disrupting malicious uses of AI: October 2025 | OpenAI (https://openai.com/global-affairs/disrupting-malicious-uses-of-ai-october-2025/)\nciteturn2638search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; October 7, 2025\n\nOctober 7, 2025\n\nGlobal Affairs\n\n# Disrupting malicious uses of AI: October 2025\n\nOur mission is to ensure that artificial general intelligence benefits all of humanity. We advance this mission by deploying innovations that help people solve difficult problems and by building democratic AI grounded in common-sense rules that protect people from real harms.\n\nSince we began our public threat reporting in February 2024, we’ve disrupted and reported over 40 networks that violated our usage policies. This includes preventing uses of AI by authoritarian regimes to control populations or coerce other states, as well as abuses like scams, malicious cyber activity, and covert influence operations.\n\nIn this update, we share case studies from the past quarter and how we’re detecting and disrupting malicious use of our models. We continue to see threat actors bolt AI onto old playbooks to move faster, not gain novel offensive capability from our models. When activity violates our policies, we ban accounts and, where appropriate, share insights with partners. Our public reporting, policy enforcement, and collaboration with peers aim to raise awareness of abuse while improving protections for everyday users.\n\n  * Read the full report(opens in a new window)\n\n## Authors\n\nBen Nimmo, Kimo Bumanglag, Michael Flossman, Nathaniel Hartley, Jack Stubbs, Albert Zhang--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVirtual Event: Disrupting Malicious Uses of AI - Event | OpenAI Forum (https://forum.openai.com/public/events/virtual-event-disrupting-malicious-uses-of-ai-xmv3xd7zlq)\nciteturn2638search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 2 days ago; October 7, 2025 ... He was a co-founder of the Atlantic Council’s Digital Forensic Research Lab (DFRLab), and later served as Graphika’s first head of investigations, and as global lead of threat intelligence at Meta.\n\nImage: Virtual Event: Disrupting Malicious Uses of AI\n\nLIVESTREAM\n\n10:00 PM - 10:40 PM GMT\n\nOctober 7, 2025\n\n# Virtual Event: Disrupting Malicious Uses of AI\n\nReplay\n\n# About the Event:\n\nIn this conversation moderated by CNN’s Jim Sciutto, OpenAI’s Ben Nimmo will share highlights from the company’s latest threat intelligence report, which examines emerging tactics used by online covert influence operations and coordinated attempts to misuse AI. The discussion will explore what OpenAI’s Intelligence and Investigations team is seeing across the threat landscape and how these insights can enable stronger defenses online.\n\n# About the Speakers:\n\n### Ben Nimmo, Principal Investigator @ OpenAI\n\nBen Nimmo is Principal Investigator on OpenAI’s Intelligence and Investigations team. He was a co-founder of the Atlantic Council’s Digital Forensic Research Lab (DFRLab), and later served as Graphika’s first head of investigations, and as global lead of threat intelligence at Meta. He is a member of the Council on Foreign Relations and an associate fellow at Pierson College at Yale. Sciutto was appointed a Senior Fellow at Yale Jackson School of Global Affairs. Sciutto is married to Gloria Riviera-Sciutto, a journalist and podcaster. They have three children and live in Washington, DC.\n\n## Speakers\n\nImage: user's Avatar\n\nBen Nimmo\n\nPrincipal Investigator @ OpenAI\n\nView Profile\n\nImage: user's Avatar\n\nJim Sciutto\n\nAnchor & Chief National Security Analyst @ CNN\n\nView Profile\n\n## Agenda\n\nFrom 10:00 PM\n\n-\n\nTo 10:05 PM\n\nGMT\n\nTags:\n\nOpening\n\nOpening Remarks\n\nFrom 10:05 PM\n\n-\n\nTo 10:35 PM\n\nGMT\n\nTags:\n\nFireside Chat\n\nFireside Chat\n\nSpeakers:\n\nImage: user's Avatar\n\nImage: user's Avatar\n\nFrom 10:35 PM\n\n-\n\nTo 10:40 PM\n\nGMT\n\nTags:\n\nClosing\n\nClosing Remarks\n\nEvent has finished\n\n10:00 PM - 10:40 PM GMT\n\nOctober 7, 2025\n\nOnline\n\nOrganized by\n\nImage: user's Avatar\n\nOpenAI Forum--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKruel.ai KV2.0 - KX (experimental research) to current 8.2- Api companion co-pilot system with full modality , understanding with persistent memory - #433 by darcschnider - Community - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/kruel-ai-kv2-0-kx-experimental-research-to-current-8-2-api-companion-co-pilot-system-with-full-modality-understanding-with-persistent-memory/674592/433)\nciteturn2638search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 11 months ago; darcschnider October 7, 2025, 1:35pm 433\n\ndarcschnider October 7, 2025, 1:35pm 433\n\n## Image: :brain: Lynda Laptop’s Long Night: A Tale of Recovery and Resilience\n\nWell… not exactly the morning we wanted. During what should’ve been a routine update, one wrong copy command decided to go rogue — and overwrote the wrong server. Image: :grimacing:\n\nAs a result, Lynda Laptop is currently out of commission. We’re restoring her to a previous checkpoint from last month. The good news? The data and indexes are intact. The not-so-good news? The memory system and indexes are out of sync, and rebuilding that link is tricky business. We’ve only managed to do this successfully once before — all other recovery attempts didn’t quite bring Lynda Prime back online.\n\nSo, we’re hopeful this time around. If not, it’s going to be a few long nights rebuilding memory from the ground up.\n\n### What Happened\n\nTotally my fault (Ben here Image: :man_raising_hand:). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLaTeX Integration for ChatGPT - Feature requests - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/latex-integration-for-chatgpt/1120842)\nciteturn2638search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.6 years ago; Crawled: last year;   * Academia & Research: Improved usability for mathematical and scientific discussions. ... Chronoak October 7, 2025, 3:08pm 2\n\n# LaTeX Integration for ChatGPT\n\nChatGPT Feature requests\n\ndanchess February 15, 2025, 2:45am 1\n\n### Summary\n\nImplement LaTeX rendering in ChatGPT to improve its utility for technical users handling mathematical and scientific notation.\n\n### Proposed Features\n\n  * Inline & Block Rendering: Native MathJax/KaTeX support for real-time equation display.\n  * Full LaTeX Document Handling: Support for structured LaTeX (sections, citations, figures, tables).\n  * Syntax Validation: Real-time LaTeX error detection and correction.\n  * Export Options: Direct output to `.tex` and `.pdf` .\n  * Interoperability: Seamless integration with Markdown and Jupyter environments.\n\n### Technical Considerations\n\n  * Rendering Engine: MathJax/KaTeX for client-side processing.\n  * Performance: Optimized handling of complex LaTeX expressions.\n  * Security: Sanitize inputs to prevent LaTeX injection vulnerabilities.\n  * Scalability: Ensure robust processing under high-load conditions.\n\n### Expected Impact\n\n  * Academia & Research: Improved usability for mathematical and scientific discussions.\n  * STEM Education: Enhanced support for students and educators.\n  * Developer & Technical Communities: Streamlined documentation workflows.\n\n### Implementation Plan\n\n  1. Prototype: Enable inline equation rendering for basic LaTeX expressions.\n  2. Expansion: Add structured document support and advanced syntax handling.\n  3. User Testing & Refinement: Gather developer feedback and iterate.\n\nLaTeX is a fundamental tool for technical communication—integrating it into ChatGPT is both necessary and expected. Let’s move forward.\n\nChronoak October 7, 2025, 3:08pm 2\n\nAgree with support latex in chat gpt to the environment and chatting page--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWhile loop is iterating 