 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: last year; tylersuard October 7, 2025, 10:35pm 1\n\n# Why does GPT-5 ignore my prompts?\n\nAPI\n\ngpt-5\n\ntylersuard October 7, 2025, 10:35pm 1\n\nHello. I spent a few months working with o1 to write an advanced document transformation program. o1 handles all the prompting like a champ. However, when I put those exact same prompts into GPT-5 it fails miserably, even on high reasoning effort. What’s even worse, GPT-5 completely ignores my instructions. I am trying to modify my o1 prompt to work with GPT-5, but the changes that I make just get ignored.\n\nWhat is going on here, and how do I fix it?\n\naprendendo.next October 7, 2025, 10:43pm 2\n\nWelcome to the forum.\n\nYes, gpt-5 prompting is a bit different from previous models.\n\nHave a look at these articles, they might help you a bit:\n\n  * GPT-5 prompting guide\n  * GPT-5 Troubleshooting Guide\n\nPaulBellow October 7, 2025, 10:44pm 3\n\nI’ve found the GPT-5 prompt optimizer helpful for a few things.\n\nhttps://platform.openai.com/chat/edit?prompt=\n\nYou will likely need to adapt the prompts, though.\n\nWere the old ones reasoning too?\n\njeffvpace October 7, 2025, 11:51pm 4\n\nDepending on your prompt, the verbosity parameter (default medium) may be problematic.\n\nFor example, we translate entire documents and have to set verbosity to low.\n\nninazane October 8, 2025, 10:28am 5\n\nBasically, your old o1 prompt isn’t clicking with GPT-5 because the two models think in different ways.\n\n  * o1 is great at reading between the lines — it reasons deeply and fills in gaps.\n\n  * GPT-5 is more literal — it wants clear, structured, no-nonsense instructions.\n\nSo when you reuse your o1 prompt, GPT-5 either ignores parts of it or gets confused.\n\nHere’s how to fix it:\n\n  1. Put your must-follow rules in the system message, not the user prompt.\n\n  2. Use structured outputs (like JSON mode or function calling) instead of hoping it formats things right.\n\n  3. Set temperature = 0 so it stays consistent.\n\n  4. Give it a tiny example of what you want.\n\n  5. Don’t rely on hidden “reasoning” — spell out the steps you expect.\n\nIn short: make your prompt more explicit, structured, and strict, and GPT-5 will follow along just fine.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIssue when try to use any of MCP servers or connectors in Agent Builder - Bugs - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/issue-when-try-to-use-any-of-mcp-servers-or-connectors-in-agent-builder/1361356)\nciteturn2638search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 10 months ago; pawlek October 7, 2025, 1:03pm 1\n\n# Issue when try to use any of MCP servers or connectors in Agent Builder\n\nAPI Bugs\n\nmcp, agent-builder\n\npawlek October 7, 2025, 1:03pm 1\n\nSo when I try to make connection with MCP server I get\n\n”**We experienced an error while running the workflow. Sorry about that! You can retry your request, or contact us through our help center at help.openai.com if you keep seeing this error. (Please include the request ID 56e54e68-4624-4c90-b1e2-318c78321784 in your message.)”\n**\nAfter that I try to use connectors and I get same issue.\n\nI have verified organisation in profile.\n\nElijah_Bare October 7, 2025, 1:12pm 2\n\nI am getting an issue like this with my custom GPT connections. They were working just fine before but now cannot even hit the health endpoint on my API.\n\nMichael_Hunger October 7, 2025, 1:20pm 3\n\nYeah I’m getting the same with our hosted MCP server.\nSadly there is no further debug information or insights where / how / why the agent failed with a simple task.\n\nElijah_Bare October 7, 2025, 1:59pm 4\n\nit would be nice if there was a human i could talk to about this lol. I hope this gets figured out soon.\n\nNatiCha October 7, 2025, 2:54pm 5\n\nI’m running into the same issue when using an MCP server tool inside an agent node. I’ve also tried running a standalone MCP server node, but the preview freezes there too — no error message or explanation.\n\nNot sure if it’s connected, but I noticed on status.openai.com that ChatGPT connectors are currently showing partial outages. Could be related.\n\nSimrell October 8, 2025, 6:06am 6\n\nAlso running into this same issue. Can occassionally get certain MCP servers to work on first run, however after refreshing page seems to be unable to fetch available tools for any given MCP tool and shows the API token I previously entered as ‘REDACTED’ … if I delete the node and re-add credentials to connect to remote server, it works again (most of the time) on first run.\n\novidiu.liuta October 8, 2025, 8:44am 7\n\nScreenshot 2025-10-08 at 11.33.18652×232 5.7 KB\n\nA quote is being added on my end after the initial MCP save; so a second tools load on the saved MCP endpoint shows the quotes in front of Bearer\n\nThis looks like a bug on Agent Builder end related to MCP tools\n\nunmahida October 8, 2025, 12:29pm 8\n\nI have hosted a hello world category FastMCP server, i was able to add it to a MCP Node as remote server but it seems when the agent works through the node its not able to get a response back.\nWhen i put the MCP server into the agent by adding a tool i am getting “We experienced an error while running the workflow.”\n\nWas anyone was able to add a remote mcp server to a MCP node or to Agent node successfully ?\nif so Any details on what kind of Mcp server ? http sse , http streaming, stdio ?\n\nNatiCha October 8, 2025, 7:17pm 9\n\nThis was super helpful. On the MCP server side, the temporary workaround is to wrap the auth token in double quotes like this:\n    \n    `auth_token = '\"temp_auth_token\"'\n    \n    `\n\nSince initialization is forcing quotes, this allows the MCP tools to load properly.\n\nOnce initialization is complete, go back to the MCP server and change it to:\n    \n    `auth_token = 'temp_auth_token'\n    \n    `\n\nso it works correctly at runtime — the runtime chat preview isn’t using wrapping quotes (`“ ”`).\n\nFor context, I’m using the Access Token / API Key dropdown option (which, confusingly, is labeled API Key / Auth Token in the modal — the labels swap order and wording depending on the context). I’m not using custom headers.\n\n> Image: :warning: OpenAI — please fix this bug!\n> The initialization step shouldn’t require wrapping the auth/access token in extra quotes for MCP initialization and tool loading to work.\n\nailifestyleadmn October 9, 2025, 12:56pm 10\n\nGood find and callout. I can replicate this behaviouras well Initialization is fine. When i set the key to never and no auth it goes to a point where for tool call it is explicitly asking for approval and fails there.\n\nFelipe_Cocco October 9, 2025, 2:25pm 11\n\nRunning into this as well with any of the Google MCP connectors. It should in theory work solely from the Oauth playground token generator, yes?\n\nNatiCha October 10, 2025, 9:36pm 12\n\nThe bug has been resolved! I removed the MCP tool and added it back — voilà, it’s working.\n\n> Note: Simply updating the auth/access token in the existing tool didn’t work. When previewing in chat, it didn’t recognize the connection and ignored the tool. Removing and re-adding the tool completely fixed the issue.\n\ntjsawyer October 14, 2025, 8:46am 13\n\nSame issue for me: Workflow failed: Reauthentication required. (code: server_error)\n\nWorks fine under “My agent” - Tools though…the node itself seems bugged though\n\nDevanshu_Srivastava October 16, 2025, 7:24am 14\n\nimage728×222 8.35 KB\n\nWhile adding the MCP using custom header option with Authorization Header , it works ok .\n\nWhen i earlier added MCP with APi Key option it did connected to list tool but was giving error while execution\n\ngabrial50 November 3, 2025, 6:09pm 15\n\nWhen connecting ChatGPT’s Agent Builder to an external MCP server (for example, the n8n MCP Server Trigger), the agent never appears to send the required initialize JSON-RPC call to begin the MCP session.\n\nThis means the server never issues an Mcp-Session-Id, and subsequent RPC calls such as tools/list fail or time out.\n\nIf you manually initialize the session first (for example, with curl or Postman), the MCP server works perfectly.\n\ncurl -i -X POST “YOUR-DOMAIN”\n-H “Accept: application/json, text/event-stream”\n-H “Content-Type: application/json”\n-d ‘{\n“jsonrpc”: “2.0”,\n“id”: “1”,\n“method”: “initialize”,\n“params”: {\n“protocolVersion”: “2025-06-18”,\n“clientInfo”: {“name”: “curl”,“version”: “8”},\n“capabilities”: {“experimental”: {“streamingResponses”: true}}\n}\n}’\n\n"
